      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2012 Wolters Kluwer Health | Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Carboxylesterase 1 (CES1) is the primary enzyme responsible for converting clopidogrel into biologically inactive carboxylic acid metabolites."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We genotyped a functional variant in CES1, G143E, in participants of the Pharmacogenomics of Anti-Platelet Intervention (PAPI) study (n=566) and in 350 patients with coronary heart disease treated with clopidogrel, and carried out an association analysis of bioactive metabolite levels, on-clopidogrel ADP-stimulated platelet aggregation, and cardiovascular outcomes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The levels of clopidogrel active metabolite were significantly greater in CES1 143E-allele carriers (P=0.001). Consistent with these findings, individuals who carried the CES1 143E-allele showed a better clopidogrel response as measured by ADP-stimulated platelet aggregation in both participants of the PAPI study (P=0.003) and clopidogrel-treated coronary heart disease patients (P=0.03). No association was found between this single nucleotide polymorphism and baseline measures of platelet aggregation in either cohort."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Taken together, these findings suggest, for the first time, that genetic variation in CES1 may be an important determinant of the efficacy of clopidogrel."
          }
        ]
      },
